// Class A. The program NAMES a `StringBuilder` and never puts a string into one.
//
// The reference emits `#include <string.h>` and a `Slice<u8>` declaration for it,
// because its typed program carries the prelude methods of a named type and those
// methods call `cstr_len`. Its own artefact then uses neither: no `strlen`, no
// `memset`, no `memcpy`, and the struct is declared once and referenced nowhere.
// Its own comment says the include is "pulled in only when something in the
// program needs it", and here nothing does.
//
// The port materialises prelude methods on demand, so it emits neither. The gate
// beside this file asserts the difference is EXACTLY those two lines and that the
// reference's own output still fails to use them - the day it needs them, the
// justification is gone and the gate goes red.
struct P { x: int }

impl Display for P {
    fn fmt(*const self, _out: *StringBuilder) { }
}

fn main() {
    let p = P { x: 5 };
    println(p.x);
}
